  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "compare people and things (más que, tan como, el mejor de)",
        "use the full set of negative and positive words (nada, nadie, nunca, ningún, ni ... ni)",
        "choose between por and para in the most common situations",
        "express obligation (tener que, hay que, deber) and use the right preposition after common verbs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. Comparisons"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.8,
        6.2,
        6.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Structure",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "más + adjective + que",
          "Madrid es más grande que Sevilla.",
          "Madrid is bigger than Seville."
        ],
        [
          "menos + adjective + que",
          "Este libro es menos caro que ese.",
          "This book is cheaper than that one."
        ],
        [
          "tan + adjective + como",
          "Ella es tan alta como su padre.",
          "She is as tall as her father."
        ],
        [
          "más / menos + noun + que",
          "Tengo más libros que tú.",
          "I have more books than you."
        ],
        [
          "tanto/a/os/as + noun + como",
          "Tengo tantos libros como tú.",
          "I have as many books as you."
        ],
        [
          "verb + más / menos que",
          "Trabajo más que antes.",
          "I work more than before."
        ],
        [
          "verb + tanto como",
          "Estudio tanto como mi hermano.",
          "I study as much as my brother."
        ],
        [
          "más / menos de + number",
          "Hay más de cien personas.",
          "There are more than a hundred people."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "warn",
      "title": "que vs de",
      "text": "Use <b>que</b> to compare two things. Use <b>de</b> instead of <i>que</i> before a <b>number</b>: <i>más de diez</i>, <i>menos de cinco euros</i>. <b>Tanto</b> agrees with the noun: tanto dinero, tanta agua, tantos libros, tantas cosas."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.0,
        4.0,
        4.0,
        5.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Adjective",
        "Comparative",
        "Superlative",
        "Example"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "bueno / bien",
          "mejor",
          "el / la mejor",
          "Es la mejor idea."
        ],
        [
          "malo / mal",
          "peor",
          "el / la peor",
          "Fue el peor día."
        ],
        [
          "grande (age)",
          "mayor",
          "el / la mayor",
          "Mi hermana es mayor que yo."
        ],
        [
          "pequeño (age)",
          "menor",
          "el / la menor",
          "Soy el menor de tres hermanos."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>mejor</b> and <b>peor</b> are used for quality: <i>Este vino es mejor que ese.</i> Do not say <i>más bueno</i>.",
        "<b>mayor</b> and <b>menor</b> are used for age: <i>mi hermano mayor</i> (my older brother). For size use <b>más grande</b> and <b>más pequeño</b>.",
        "Equal quality: <i>igual de + adjective + que</i>: <i>Es igual de rápido que tú.</i>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. Superlatives"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The most / the least: <b>el / la / los / las + más / menos + adjective + de</b>. The word after the superlative is <b>de</b>, not <i>in</i>."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        7.4,
        5.0,
        4.6
      ],
      "head": [
        "Example",
        "English",
        "Note"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Es la ciudad más grande de España.",
          "It is the biggest city in Spain.",
          "de, not en"
        ],
        [
          "Es el alumno más inteligente de la clase.",
          "He is the cleverest pupil in the class.",
          ""
        ],
        [
          "Son los coches menos caros de la tienda.",
          "They are the cheapest cars in the shop.",
          ""
        ],
        [
          "Este es el mejor restaurante del barrio.",
          "This is the best restaurant in the area.",
          "de + el = del"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "-ísimo: very, very",
      "text": "Add <b>-ísimo</b> (drop the final vowel) for an extreme degree: <i>grande</i> gives <b>grandísimo</b>, <i>caro</i> gives <b>carísimo</b>, <i>muy bueno</i> gives <b>buenísimo</b>, <i>rico</i> gives <b>riquísimo</b> (c becomes qu). It agrees: <i>buenísima, buenísimos</i>."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Dutch dan, als and in",
      "text": "<i>groter dan</i> = <b>más grande que</b>. <i>zo groot als</i> = <b>tan grande como</b>. <i>de grootste in Spanje</i> = <b>el más grande de España</b>."
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "3. Negative and positive words"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        3.2,
        3.2,
        3.2,
        3.2,
        4.2
      ],
      "head": [
        "Positive",
        "Negative",
        "Positive",
        "Negative",
        "Example"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "algo",
          "nada",
          "alguien",
          "nadie",
          "No veo nada. No veo a nadie."
        ],
        [
          "siempre",
          "nunca / jamás",
          "también",
          "tampoco",
          "Nunca llego tarde. Yo tampoco."
        ],
        [
          "algún / alguno",
          "ningún / ninguno",
          "o ... o",
          "ni ... ni",
          "No tengo ningún problema."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>After the verb</b>, you need <b>no</b> in front: <i>No veo nada.</i> <b>Before the verb</b>, no <i>no</i>: <i>Nada veo. Nadie habla. Nunca llego tarde.</i>",
        "<b>ningún</b> / <b>ninguna</b> is used with a singular noun, even when the idea is plural: <i>No tengo ningún amigo aquí. No hay ninguna solución.</i> <b>Ninguno</b> stands alone: <i>¿Tienes hermanos? No, ninguno.</i>",
        "<b>ni ... ni</b>: <i>No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero.</i> / <i>Ni él ni ella hablan francés.</i>",
        "<b>jamás</b> is a stronger <i>nunca</i>: <i>Jamás lo olvidaré.</i>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "pagebreak"
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "4. Por and para"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Both can mean \"for\", which makes them hard for Dutch speakers (<i>voor</i>). The basic idea: <b>para</b> points forward to a goal, a destination or a recipient. <b>Por</b> looks back to the cause or passes through."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        2.0,
        5.0,
        5.4,
        4.6
      ],
      "head": [
        "",
        "Use",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "para",
          "purpose (in order to)",
          "Estudio para aprender.",
          "I study in order to learn."
        ],
        [
          "para",
          "recipient",
          "Este regalo es para ti.",
          "This present is for you."
        ],
        [
          "para",
          "destination",
          "Salgo para Madrid.",
          "I am leaving for Madrid."
        ],
        [
          "para",
          "deadline",
          "Lo necesito para el lunes.",
          "I need it by Monday."
        ],
        [
          "para",
          "opinion",
          "Para mí, es muy difícil.",
          "For me, it is very difficult."
        ],
        [
          "para",
          "employer",
